From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: B Honey <9922368+Mhsbrian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 10:49:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 18/23] nak: reuse a block's copy of an immediate instead of
 copying it again

When an instruction cannot take an immediate where it has one, for
example the second immediate of ffma x * a + b, the legalizer copies it
into a register right before that instruction. It did so for every
instruction, also when the same value had just been copied for the one
before. In a compute loop of 32 independent ffma chains with the same two
constants, every ffma got its own mov of the addend and a read-after-write
wait on it: 64 instructions issued for 32 ffma.

Keep the block's copies of 32-bit immediates, by register file and value,
and let a source reuse one made at most 64 instructions earlier. Past
that window the immediate is copied again as before, so a copy never
keeps a register live across a long stretch of the block just to save a
mov. 64-bit immediates are left alone.

On an RTX 4090 (NVK on NVIDIA's kernel driver, Android bionic) that loop
went from 2274 to 734 static cycles and from 39.7 to 56-57 TFLOPS; NVIDIA's
own driver ran it at 64-75 TFLOPS at the same clocks. dEQP-VK, 66,226
cases: the same result for every case before and after.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
